WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:08.600
A farm co-op handles about 39 tonnes of manure, silage and straw a day.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:19.600
Composting it releases heat. This unit is designed to capture it: three insulated tunnels, 988 m³ in all.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:32.600
Blowers push air up through a spigot floor. The bed stays aerobic and heats itself to 55–70 °C.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:45.600
The hot, saturated exhaust, 7,030 m³ an hour at 60 °C, is collected from the headspace.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:53.800
On 216 m² of plate-fin coils it cools to 35 °C. 92 % of that heat is water vapour condensing.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:01.600
Water leaves at 50 °C for the farm's heat users. The condensate goes back to wet the beds.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:17.600
The tunnels start five days apart, so one is always heating, one is hot and one is cooling. Every five days one is emptied and refilled.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:27.600
The compost process comes first. Temperature, oxygen and sanitisation set the airflow; heat recovery takes what is left.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:37.400
Indicative design from an open energy model. Nothing at this scale has been built yet.
